# Treat data and model weights as binary/generated so they don't bloat diffs
*.csv linguist-generated=true
*.json linguist-generated=true
*.pth binary
*.joblib binary
*.pt binary

# Explicitly ensure markdown files and rules are treated as text
*.md text
.antigravityrules text

# Freeze-checked dataset must stay LF so its SHA-256 is identical across platforms/CI
data/immunogenicity_dataset_v3.csv text eol=lf

# Same reason, generalised 2026-08-12: any artifact whose sha256 is RECORDED in a
# provenance sidecar must be byte-identical on every platform, or the integrity
# harness's provenance check FAILs on a file that is perfectly correct. Measured
# on this box: results/calibration_assessment_v5_mode31.csv is 1,712 bytes with
# 13 CRs in the Windows working tree and 1,699 bytes with 0 CRs as a git blob,
# because core.autocrlf=true rewrites on checkout. A hash taken from the working
# tree is therefore a Windows-only hash. Pinning eol=lf makes the two agree.
results/*.csv text eol=lf
results/*.provenance.json text eol=lf
# Added 2026-08-12 (accuracy_science_plan item 3): a benchmark script's PRIMARY
# .json output can also carry a recorded self-hash now (see
# src.artifact_integrity.write_provenance_sidecar), not just its sidecar - the
# provenance.json glob above does not cover it. Same eol=lf reasoning applies.
results/*.json text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-12 (continuous_validation provenance): results/*.json above is
# a single-segment glob and does NOT match paths one directory deeper - verified
# with `git check-attr eol -- results/continuous_validation/benchmark_latest.json`,
# which returned "eol: unspecified" before this line was added.
# src/continuous_validation.py writes its benchmark JSON and provenance
# sidecars under results/continuous_validation/, so that subtree needs its own
# eol=lf pin or the sha256 recorded in each sidecar would be a Windows-only
# hash, same failure mode as the incident referenced above.
results/continuous_validation/*.json text eol=lf
results/continuous_validation/*.provenance.json text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-13 (D24-resid, open-item register): the same failure mode, one
# directory earlier in the chain. scripts/assess_calibration.py records this
# file's OWN sha256 as `oof_sha256` in results/calibration_assessment_v5_mode31.csv's
# provenance sidecar, but models/v5/*.csv had no eol pin, so that hash was taken
# from the Windows-CRLF working tree (331b00a8...) rather than the actual LF git
# blob (4862bd59...) a Linux clone or CI checkout would see - it would never
# reproduce there. Scoped to this one file, not a models/v5/*.csv glob: no other
# file in that directory is currently hashed into a provenance sidecar, and a
# broader glob would risk invalidating hashes recorded elsewhere without the
# corresponding audit this fix's own sidecar update performed.
models/v5/rf_oof_predictions_mode31.csv text eol=lf

# Added 2026-08-17 (D5/E1, task_list_2026-08-17_pr_review_and_skip_audit.md): same failure mode as
# above, for the v5 dataset deposition files docs/zenodo_deposition.md hands a reader a `sha256sum -c`
# block for. Measured: the git blob for all three is already LF (0 CR), but a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true adds CRLFs back (51,186 of them in the CSV alone), so the "verify before upload"
# instructions produced a Windows-only hash that a Linux clone (or CI) could never reproduce.
data/immunogenicity_dataset_v5.csv text eol=lf
data/immunogenicity_dataset_v5_schema.json text eol=lf
data/immunogenicity_dataset_v5_provenance.json text eol=lf

# Extended per D5 (same task list): pin the broader unchecksummed-artifact globs first, so any
# provenance sidecar backfilled against them later records a portable hash rather than a Windows one.
data/proteomes/*.fasta eol=lf
data/allele_aware/*.csv eol=lf
data/allele_aware/*_provenance.json eol=lf
models/peptide_binding_matrix_v*.csv eol=lf
